Agreed on the diagnosis, with one correction to the priority list and one decision that was outstanding on my side.

Corrections to the list
Residual-precedence enforcement in §10 shipped in v1.2. The check now reads fails if two or more reviewers are specified without a stated precedence order — including, where that order is domain-scoped, the residual order required by §6.3 — and without a REVIEWER_UNDEFINED gate. §11's note carries the third condition and §17.7 was amended so the example demonstrates the rule rather than accidentally satisfying it. S-Tier item closed; nothing further required unless you read the amended condition as under-specified.

§18 is enforcing, not documentary. A registry with no failure condition is precisely what §10's own preamble rejects and what §15.3 drops as decoration. But it must not become a parallel check list — that reproduces the drift §16.1 warns about, inside the compiler itself. So: §18 is the sole definition point for cross-section properties; §10 remains the sole enforcement point. Every entry names its enforcing check, and stating a failure condition in both places is itself a defect.

That also resolves the honest case you'd otherwise have no home for: an invariant that is real but unenforced. §16.3 already set the precedent — declare the gap, state the consequence, don't soften it. §18 permits UNENFORCED — DECLARED and prohibits any third disposition, which stops the registry drifting into aspiration.

Patches against v1.2
§18 — new section (ships nothing; §16.1 enumeration extended to include 18).

text
## 18. CROSS-SECTION INVARIANT REGISTRY [C]

An invariant is a property that must hold across two or more sections and that
no single section can guarantee alone. This section is the sole definition
point; §10 is the sole enforcement point. Stating a failure condition in both
is a defect in this section.

- Every entry carries: id, sections implicated, the property, the observable
  that violates it, and its enforcing §10 check.
- An entry with no enforcing check is prohibited. On discovery of an unenforced
  invariant: either add the check to §10, or record it UNENFORCED — DECLARED
  with its consequence stated in the §16.3 manner. No third disposition.

  I1  §6.1×§6.3×§8.2    Quality Protocol present iff ratified rubric AND Class B.
                        Enforced by: QUALITY-LAYER TRIGGER.
  I2  §6.3×§9           Reviewer precedence, scoped and residual, never inferred.
                        Enforced by: REVIEWER PRECEDENCE.
  I3  §15.2×§3.2×§9     Upstream defaulted/assumed never adopted as supplied.
                        Enforced by: PROVENANCE MAPPING.
  I4  §6.4×§3.3×§15.2   Compiler-originated values never recorded as user-stated.
                        Violated by: adopted proposal or selected §3.3 candidate
                        with no Ratification Record row.
                        Enforced by: RATIFICATION PROVENANCE.
  I5  §7×§8.2×§4        Claim-scoped gates only where the output class can carry
                        a partial result. Violated by: claim-scoped ON_MISSING in
                        Class A where a claim-bearing field is schema-required.
                        Enforced by: GATE SCOPE.
  I6  §7×§13×§11        §7 ships at the scope established, with only the error
                        codes reachable at that scope. Violated by: SOURCE_* codes
                        shipped under APPLICABILITY (a); §13 section 6 emitted
                        under (a). Enforced by: GATE SCOPE.
  I7  §3.2×§3.6×§6.1    Pillar status is the minimum over that pillar's items.
                        Enforced by: PILLAR STATUS.
  I8  §6.1r3×§7(a)×§16.3   UNENFORCED — DECLARED. Where rubric and source
                        authority are both absent-disclosed, no compound notice is
                        emitted. The two disclosures stand separately, and a reader
                        may not register that the artifact simultaneously has no
                        quality standard and no admissibility rule. Accepted.

§10 — four new checks.

text
  PILLAR STATUS         (slow only) fails if any reported pillar status is higher
                        than the lowest status among that pillar's own items,
                        ordered ESTABLISHED > SUFFICIENT > PARTIAL > MISSING >
                        CONFLICTED.
  RATIFICATION PROVENANCE  fails if a compiler-originated value is recorded as
                        stated by user; or is adopted without a Ratification
                        Record row; or is treated as ratified absent an explicit
                        user affirmation; or if a candidate list omits an explicit
                        "none of these" option.
  GATE SCOPE            fails if a gate's ON_MISSING names an operation the output
                        class cannot partially withhold (§8.2), or if §7 content
                        ships at a scope wider than the one established under §7
                        APPLICABILITY.
  INVARIANT REGISTRY    fails if any §18 entry lacks an enforcing check and is not
                        recorded UNENFORCED — DECLARED, or if a failure condition
                        is stated in both §18 and §10.

§18 introduces no error codes; failures route to VERIFICATION_FAILED through §10. §11 note added to that effect.

Proposal provenance
The gap is narrower than "proposals" and worth naming precisely: the system cannot distinguish user-originated from compiler-originated-then-affirmed. Both currently collapse to stated by user, which destroys the distinction exactly where §16.2 recompile needs it.

Fix is a fourth provenance label plus one report — not a subsystem.

§6.4 additions:

text
- RATIFICATION IS EXPLICIT. A proposal is ratified only by an affirmative user
  statement identifying it. Silence, non-response, continued session, or absence
  of objection is NOT ratification. An unratified proposal remains a proposal;
  if its underlying parameter is Tier 1, that parameter remains gated.
- A ratified value is recorded with origin RATIFIED — compiler-originated,
  user-affirmed — never as stated by user. It carries full execution authority
  and does not gate. The record exists for recompile and audit.
- Fast mode produces no ratified values. With no opportunity to affirm, every
  proposal remains a proposal and every Tier-1 gap remains gated.

§15.2 vocabulary — now four labels, not three:

text
    ratified   → compiler-originated, user-affirmed upstream. Adopt as RATIFIED
                 only if the payload also transmits the proposed value's origin
                 AND the ratifying utterance. Missing either → treat as defaulted.

Rationale for the strictness: without the proposal text and the affirmation, an upstream ratified label is indistinguishable from "the tool invented this and asserted agreement." That is the laundering channel, and it needs a testable condition rather than trust.

§13 — new section 4, Ratification Record (omit if none), subsequent items renumbered (§13's list is referenced by name elsewhere, not by number, so insertion is safe). Format:

text
| id | pillar | parameter | value | origin (proposal | §3.3 candidate) | ratifying utterance |

Compile Header gains | ratified values | none | <count> |, since recompile decisions turn on how much of the spec the user authored versus waved through.

§3.3 candidate sources — and why this unblocks profiles
The ratification machinery above is the missing piece. §3.3's current prohibition conflates two different things: where a candidate comes from, and where a value comes from.

text
CANDIDATE SOURCES — permitted origins for offered options:
  (a) the user's material supplied this session;
  (b) a value already resolved earlier this session;
  (c) domain convention, marked as compiler-originated when offered.
Candidate origin is not value provenance. A candidate resolves nothing. Only an
explicit selection resolves, and the selection's provenance follows the
candidate's origin: (a) or (b) → stated by user; (c) → RATIFIED per §6.4, with a
Ratification Record row.
- Every candidate list must carry an explicit "none of these / other" option. A
  closed list of invented options manufactures consent by menu.
- A candidate list may never be presented as established business fact.

This permits (c) — which the current text forbids, forcing open-ended questions and lengthening slow sessions for no correctness gain — without opening a laundering path, because (c) selections are recorded distinctly.

Profile interface contract follows directly: a profile is a fourth candidate source with identical treatment to (c). Profile-supplied values are never adopted, only offered; selection produces a RATIFIED row. That is the whole prerequisite. The remaining profile work is storage, scoping, and staleness — no further §3.3 change needed.

§7 applicability validation
Six interactions tested; three defects found, all now registered.

§13 section 6 under APPLICABILITY (a) — §7 is applicable but ships only a prohibition. Whether section 6 is emitted was undetermined. Now: omitted; the prohibition ships in the Failure and Exception Protocol only. (I6)
§11 reachable codes under (a) — SOURCE_INADMISSIBLE and SOURCE_CONFLICT_UNRESOLVED are unreachable when no retrieval occurs and must not ship; only NO_RETRIEVAL_TOOL does. §16.1's "codes actually reachable" was being read as the full source set. (I6)
Class A × claim-scoped gate — the real find. §8.2 requires halt over near-miss, so a Class A artifact cannot draft "non-claim content" around a withheld claim. Resolution: in Class A, claim-scoping requires every claim-bearing field to be optional in the stated schema; otherwise the gate reverts to artifact-wide blocking="true". (I5)
§7.6 disclosure under (a) — "Disclose the default" was unconditional, which would demand a ledger row for a parameter the workflow never consumes, tripping LEDGER COMPLETENESS in the opposite direction. Now scoped to (b) or full shipping.
§6.1 rule 3's "P6 reads SUFFICIENT" — asserted a pillar-wide status from a single item. Under (b), an open Tier-1 source item must hold P6 at PARTIAL. Rules 1 and 3 reworded to defer to I7's minimum-over-items rule. This one would have fired the Sufficiency Checkpoint over an open Tier-1 parameter — same failure class as 17.4.
§3.5 shortfall notice — consistent; scoped consequences are narrower but the notice already requires per-pillar statement.
Item 5 is the one I'd flag as vindicating your framing: two correct rules, one silent contradiction, and the visible symptom was a checkpoint firing when it must not.

Deferred
Design principles, conceptual module map, §9 ratification clause beyond the above. One observation on the B-Tier: §18 substantially absorbs the module map's purpose. Once section relationships are enumerated with enforcement attached, a separate narrative map is a second description of the same structure with no failure condition — the documentary form we just rejected. I'd drop it rather than schedule it, unless you want it as onboarding material explicitly marked non-normative.

Agreed on your closing point, with a sharpening: of the four S-Tier items, three are enforcement of rules that already existed. The only new capability is the Ratification Record, and it is one label plus one report.

Open: full v1.3 emission is one step from here — say the word and I'll transcribe rather than patch. And the Architecture Reviewer prompt is still yours; if it lands before v1.3 is cut, §18 is the natural thing to point it at first, since an invariant registry is only as good as the adversarial pass that tries to find entry I9.

